~ Console output lives in the core.io module (imported explicitly).
~   io.print(x)   writes x + a newline to io.stdout (any renderable value)
~   io.eprint(x)  same, to io.stderr
~   io.write(content, fd)  the rendered bytes to a file descriptor; returns the byte count
~ `io.print(x)` is the ergonomic form of `io.write(x, io.stdout)` plus the newline. On output,
~ `print` shows its value for a reader; `write` is byte-exact. Both measure a Text in bytes.
~ `<< core.test` verifies the byte count `write` returns; on success the program exits 0.
<< core.io
<< core.test

~ `io.write` is reached only through the module binding, so a program's own bare `write`
~ is a different, unrelated function — here a wrapper that fixes the descriptor. Building
~ on a module means holding it and delegating, never extending its names.
write = (content :: Text) -> Num => < io.write(content, io.stdout) >

^ = () -> $ => <
  io.print("hello")            ~ stdout: hello\n
  written :: Num = io.write("raw", io.stdout)   ~ stdout: raw   (no newline)
  assert(written, equals(3))      ~ "raw" is 3 bytes
  assert(write("!"), equals(1))   ~ the program's own wrapper, a plain call

  ~ What reaches the descriptor is bytes, not characters: "héllo" is five characters and
  ~ six bytes, and `print` writes the same six plus a newline.
  assert(write("héllo"), equals(6))
  io.print("héllo")            ~ io.stdout: héllo\n

  io.eprint("done")            ~ io.stderr: done\n
>
